  <section class='content-section'>
    <div class='topic-section'>
      <h2>The Foundation: Context Engineering</h2>
      <p class='mb-2'>Before I show you specific debugging techniques, let me share the most important thing I learned: <strong>the quality of AI's help depends entirely on the context I provide.</strong></p>

      <div class='intro-box'>
        <p><strong>🟠 HAP's Starting Point:</strong> I'm starting by learning to write better prompts. Not just "fix my code" but "fix this code that does X, is currently doing Y, and must work with Z." Every detail I add makes AI's response more useful.</p>
      </div>

      <p class='mt-2'>Grace put together a comprehensive guide on context engineering that I keep bookmarked. Here's what I've learned from it so far:</p>

      <h3 class='mt-3'>What Is Context Engineering?</h3>
      <blockquote class='mt-1' style='border-left: 3px solid var(--warm-orange); padding-left: 1rem; margin-left: 0; font-style: italic;'>
        "Context engineering is focused less on clever phrasing and more on bringing the right information (in the right format) to the LLM."
        <footer style='font-style: normal; margin-top: 0.5rem;'>— Ankur Goyal, Braintrust CEO</footer>
      </blockquote>

      <p class='mt-2'>That was my breakthrough moment. I'd been trying to write "perfect prompts" when I should have been thinking about what information AI actually needs.</p>

      <h3 class='mt-3'>The Three Types of Context</h3>
      <p class='mt-1'>Grace's guide explains that AI gets context from three sources:</p>

      <div class='overview-grid mt-2'>
        <div class='insight-card'>
          <h3><span class='insight-icon'>🔄</span> Calculated Context</h3>
          <p><strong>Automatic.</strong> Files open in your editor, your current cursor position, recently edited files. You get this without doing anything.</p>
        </div>

        <div class='insight-card'>
          <h3><span class='insight-icon'>🔍</span> Implicit Context</h3>
          <p><strong>Inferred.</strong> AI figures out patterns from your code — file extensions, naming conventions, import statements, coding style.</p>
        </div>

        <div class='insight-card'>
          <h3><span class='insight-icon'>✍️</span> Explicit Context</h3>
          <p><strong>You provide.</strong> Comments describing intent, JSDoc documentation, clear function names, instruction files. This is where context engineering happens.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3 class='mt-3'>The Five Contexts for Debugging Prompts</h3>
      <p class='mt-1'>For debugging specifically, I learned to include these five pieces of information:</p>

      <div class='overview-grid mt-2'>
        <div class='insight-card'>
          <h3><span class='insight-icon'>1️⃣</span> What It Should Do</h3>
          <p>The expected behavior. "This function should return 'Optimal' for temperatures 60-75°F."</p>
        </div>

        <div class='insight-card'>
          <h3><span class='insight-icon'>2️⃣</span> What It's Actually Doing</h3>
          <p>The current (wrong) behavior. "It's returning 'Cool' for 72°F."</p>
        </div>

        <div class='insight-card'>
          <h3><span class='insight-icon'>3️⃣</span> What I've Tried</h3>
          <p>Your debugging attempts so far. "I checked the condition order but it looks right to me."</p>
        </div>

        <div class='insight-card'>
          <h3><span class='insight-icon'>4️⃣</span> What Must Not Change</h3>
          <p>Constraints and dependencies. "Don't change the return value format — other code depends on it."</p>
        </div>

        <div class='insight-card'>
          <h3><span class='insight-icon'>5️⃣</span> Environment Details</h3>
          <p>Units, formats, versions. "Temperature is in Fahrenheit, not Celsius."</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class='hap-note-callout mt-3'>
        <div class='hap-note-content'>
          <h3>🟠 Why This Changed Everything for Me</h3>
          <p>I used to blame AI when responses weren't helpful — "it's so random!" But Prof. Teeters pointed out something important: while AI outputs vary, the quality of my prompts is one of the variables I can control. Better context doesn't guarantee perfect results, but it dramatically improves the odds.</p>
          <p class='mt-1'>There's a lot more to learn — project-level instruction files, issue-driven development, and advanced techniques I haven't tried yet. For now, I'm focusing on writing better prompts with these five contexts.</p>
          <p class='mt-2'><strong>📚 Full Reference:</strong> <a href='https://gist.github.com/cynthiateeters/a8ce39ce3d9f563be13e3fad75526d57' style='color: var(--warm-orange);' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>Grace's Context Engineering Guide</a> — everything I know about this topic came from here.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================
         SECTION 3: What AI Can and Can't Do
         Strengths and weaknesses grid
         ============================================================ -->
  <section class='content-section'>
    <div class='topic-section'>
      <h2>What AI Can and Can't Do</h2>
      <p class='mb-2'>When I first started using AI, I thought it could do everything. I'd ask it to "make my code better" and expect miracles. Prof. Teeters helped me understand that AI has specific strengths and real limitations. Knowing both helps me use it effectively.</p>

      <div class='analysis-grid mt-2'>
        <div class='analysis-point'>
          <h3>AI Excels At</h3>
          <ul class='mt-1'>
            <li><strong>Spotting syntax errors:</strong> Missing brackets, typos, incorrect operators — AI catches these instantly</li>
            <li><strong>Explaining code behavior:</strong> "What does this loop do?" gets clear, step-by-step explanations</li>
            <li><strong>Suggesting alternatives:</strong> "How else could I write this condition?" shows multiple approaches</li>
            <li><strong>Pattern recognition:</strong> AI spots common bugs like off-by-one errors and falsy value confusion</li>
            <li><strong>Refactoring suggestions:</strong> Making code cleaner while preserving behavior</li>
            <li><strong>Documenting code:</strong> Generating comments that explain what code does</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class='analysis-point'>
          <h3>AI Struggles With</h3>
          <ul class='mt-1'>
            <li><strong>Your specific context:</strong> AI doesn't know your project, your users, or your constraints</li>
            <li><strong>Accessibility requirements:</strong> AI consistently forgets WCAG unless you explicitly ask</li>
            <li><strong>Business logic:</strong> AI can't know that "premium users" need different handling</li>
            <li><strong>Performance trade-offs:</strong> AI may suggest elegant but slow solutions</li>
            <li><strong>Security implications:</strong> AI may introduce vulnerabilities it doesn't recognize</li>
            <li><strong>Edge cases in YOUR data:</strong> AI generates generic solutions, not solutions for your specific edge cases</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class='hap-note-callout mt-3'>
        <img src='https://res.cloudinary.com/cynthia-teeters/image/upload/f_auto,q_auto,w_150,c_limit/v1759495998/hap-laptop_xiewar.jpg' alt='HAP working on his laptop' width='150' height='150' class='hap-note-image' decoding='async' loading='lazy' />
        <div class='hap-note-content'>
          <h3>HAP's Takeaway: AI Teaches Best When You Ask the Right Questions</h3>
          <p>Here's what surprised me most: AI is an incredible teacher — but only when I ask the right way. When I asked "why does condition order matter?" AI gave me a step-by-step explanation I could actually understand. That's teaching at its best: immediate, focused on exactly what I needed.</p>
          <p class='mt-1'>But when I asked vague questions like "make my code better," AI would make changes I didn't understand — sometimes breaking accessibility features it didn't know were important. The difference wasn't AI's capability; it was my prompt quality.</p>
          <p class='mt-1'>Prof. Teeters put it this way: "AI can teach you anything you ask about — but it can only answer the question you actually asked. The skill isn't using AI; it's asking the right questions." 🟠</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
