  <!-- ============================================================
         SECTION 4: Debugging Truthy/Falsy Issues with AI
         Vague prompt → Bad output → Detailed prompt → Good output
         ============================================================ -->
  <section class='content-section'>
    <div class='topic-section'>
      <h2>Debugging Truthy/Falsy Issues with AI</h2>
      <p class='mb-2'>Remember Station 1, where I learned about the six falsy values? When I started debugging truthy/falsy issues with AI, I made every mistake possible. My vague prompts led to "fixes" that broke more than they fixed.</p>

      <h3 class='mt-3'>Before I Learned to Write Good Prompts</h3>
      <p class='mt-1'>I had a bug where empty strings weren't being caught by my validation. Here's how my early attempts went wrong:</p>

      <div class='warning-box mt-2'>
        <h3>❌ Vague Prompt Produces Unusable Code</h3>
        <p><strong>My vague prompt:</strong></p>
        <div class='prompt-box mt-1'>
          <div class='prompt-label'>What I Asked AI:</div>
          <CodeBlock code={`Fix my validation function - empty values aren't being caught`} lang='text' />
        </div>

        <p class='mt-2'><strong>What AI generated:</strong></p>
        <CodeBlock
          code={`function validateInput(value) {
    // Check if value is empty
    if (!value) {
        return false;
    }
    return true;
}
/* ❌ Treats 0 as invalid (but 0 might be a valid input!) */
/* ❌ Treats false as invalid (but false might be intentional!) */
/* ❌ Doesn't explain which falsy values it's catching */`}
          lang='javascript'
        />

        <p class='mt-1'><strong>Problems with this code:</strong></p>
        <ul class='mt-1'>
          <li><strong>Too aggressive</strong> — It rejects 0 and false, which might be valid inputs in my robot status system</li>
          <li><strong>No context awareness</strong> — AI didn't know I only wanted to catch empty strings and null</li>
          <li><strong>Silent failure</strong> — When my energy level was 0, the validation rejected it as "empty"</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <h3 class='mt-3'>The Prompt I Learned to Write</h3>
      <p class='mt-1'>Prof. Teeters taught me to include five key pieces of information in every debugging prompt:</p>

      <div class='prompt-box mt-2'>
        <div class='prompt-label'>✅ Detailed Prompt with All Requirements:</div>
        <CodeBlock
          code={`Debug this validation function for a robot status system:

CURRENT CODE:
function validateNickname(value) {
    if (!value) return false;
    return true;
}

THE BUG: Empty strings ("") are passing validation but should fail.

REQUIREMENTS:
- Must reject: null, undefined, empty string ""
- Must ACCEPT: 0 (valid for energyLevel), false (valid for boolean flags)
- This is for user-facing nickname input, not numeric fields
- Return false for invalid, true for valid

WHAT I'VE TRIED: Using !value catches too much - it rejects 0

Please explain why the current approach fails and provide a fix
that only targets the specific falsy values I need to reject.`}
          lang='text'
        />
      </div>

      <h3 class='mt-3'>What AI Generates with a Good Prompt</h3>
      <div class='prompt-box mt-2'>
        <div class='prompt-label'>✅ AI Output with Detailed Constraints:</div>
        <CodeBlock
          code={`function validateNickname(value) {
    // Explicitly check for the specific falsy values we want to reject
    // This preserves 0 and false as valid inputs for other fields
    if (value === null || value === undefined || value === "") {
        return false;
    }
    return true;
}

/* ✅ Rejects only null, undefined, and empty string */
/* ✅ Accepts 0 (for energyLevel) and false (for flags) */
/* ✅ Clear comments explain the reasoning */`}
          lang='javascript'
        />
        <p class='code-comment mt-1'><strong>Why this code is better:</strong> It targets exactly what I asked for. The explicit comparisons make the code self-documenting, and 0 and false work correctly for other parts of my robot status system.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================
         SECTION 5: Debugging Conditional Logic with AI
         More complex task requiring even more specificity
         ============================================================ -->
  <section class='content-section'>
    <div class='topic-section'>
      <h2>Debugging Conditional Logic with AI</h2>
      <p class='mb-2'>Conditional bugs are trickier than simple falsy issues. When my if/else chains weren't working, I learned that AI needs even MORE context to help effectively. The more complex the logic, the more detailed my prompt needs to be.</p>

      <h3 class='mt-3'>The Task: Temperature Status Classifier</h3>
      <p class='mt-1'>I had a bug where temperatures between 60-75 were showing "Cool" instead of "Optimal." The conditions looked right to me, but something was wrong with the order or the operators.</p>

      <h3 class='mt-3'>My Effective Prompt</h3>
      <div class='prompt-box mt-2'>
        <div class='prompt-label'>✅ Comprehensive Prompt:</div>
        <CodeBlock
          code={`Debug this temperature status classifier for my robot monitoring system:

CURRENT CODE:
let coreTemperature = 72;
let temperatureStatus;

if (coreTemperature > 60) {
    temperatureStatus = "Cool";
} else if (coreTemperature > 75) {
    temperatureStatus = "Optimal";
} else if (coreTemperature > 90) {
    temperatureStatus = "Warm";
} else {
    temperatureStatus = "OVERHEATING";
}

THE BUG: Temperature 72 shows "Cool" but should show "Optimal"

EXPECTED BEHAVIOR:
- Below 60: "Cool"
- 60-75: "Optimal"
- 76-90: "Warm"
- Above 90: "OVERHEATING"

TEMPERATURE UNITS: Fahrenheit (not Celsius!)

Please:
1. Explain why my current order is wrong
2. Show the correct condition order
3. Explain why order matters in if/else chains`}
          lang='text'
        />
      </div>

      <h3 class='mt-3'>AI-Generated Code</h3>
      <div class='prompt-box mt-2'>
        <div class='prompt-label'>✅ Professional Output:</div>
        <CodeBlock
          code={`/* Temperature Status Classifier - Fixed condition order */
let coreTemperature = 72;
let temperatureStatus;

// IMPORTANT: Check from highest to lowest!
// Once a condition is true, the rest are skipped.
if (coreTemperature > 90) {
    temperatureStatus = "OVERHEATING";
} else if (coreTemperature > 75) {
    temperatureStatus = "Warm";
} else if (coreTemperature > 60) {
    temperatureStatus = "Optimal";
} else {
    temperatureStatus = "Cool";
}

/* WHY THE ORIGINAL FAILED:
 * You checked > 60 FIRST, which matched 72.
 * The else if for > 75 never ran because 72 > 60 was already true!
 *
 * THE FIX: Check largest values first.
 * 72 is NOT > 90, NOT > 75, but IS > 60 → "Optimal" ✅
 */`}
          lang='javascript'
        />
        <p class='code-comment mt-1'><strong>Why this is excellent:</strong> AI didn't just fix the bug — it explained WHY it happened and how condition order affects execution. I learned something, not just got working code.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================
         SECTION 6: AI for Control Flow Accessibility
         CRITICAL: AI constantly forgets accessibility
         ============================================================ -->
  <section class='content-section'>
    <div class='topic-section'>
      <h2>AI for Control Flow Accessibility</h2>
      <p class='mb-2'><strong>This is where AI fails most consistently, and it's the most important issue to understand.</strong> When I asked AI to debug my interactive demos, it would fix the logic but silently remove accessibility features I had carefully added. Prof. Teeters says this is AI's biggest blind spot.</p>

      <h3 class='mt-3'>The Problem: AI Constantly Forgets Accessibility</h3>
      <p class='mt-1'>I asked AI to "simplify" my Skill Scanner demo. It worked beautifully — but keyboard users couldn't navigate it anymore. The AI saw the focus handling as "unnecessary complexity" and removed it.</p>

      <h3 class='mt-3'>The Solution: Make Accessibility Explicit in Every Prompt</h3>
      <div class='prompt-box mt-2'>
        <div class='prompt-label'>✅ Accessibility-First Prompt Template:</div>
        <CodeBlock
          code={`Debug this interactive loop visualization with these CRITICAL accessibility requirements:

MANDATORY accessibility features (do NOT remove or change):
- All interactive elements must be keyboard accessible
- Focus states must be visible (never remove outline styles)
- Screen reader announcements for state changes (aria-live regions)
- Color is not the only indicator (use text labels too)
- Reduced motion support for animations

CURRENT CODE:
[paste your code here]

THE BUG:
[describe what's wrong with the logic]

Fix the logic bug but preserve ALL accessibility features listed above.`}
          lang='text'
        />
      </div>

      <h3 class='mt-3'>Before and After: Accessibility Comparison</h3>

      <div class='warning-box mt-2'>
        <h3>❌ AI Code WITHOUT Accessibility Request</h3>
        <CodeBlock
          code={`// AI "simplified" version - DANGEROUS
button.addEventListener('click', () => {
    currentIndex++;
    updateDisplay();
});

function updateDisplay() {
    items.forEach((item, i) => {
        item.style.background = i === currentIndex ? '#4CAF50' : '#fff';
    });
}

/* ❌ NO keyboard support - VIOLATES WCAG */
/* ❌ NO focus management - users get lost */
/* ❌ Color-only indication - invisible to colorblind users */`}
          lang='javascript'
        />
        <p class='mt-1'><strong>This code is dangerous.</strong> It looks clean and works for mouse users, but it excludes keyboard users, screen reader users, and people with color vision differences.</p>
      </div>

      <div class='prompt-box mt-2'>
        <div class='prompt-label'>✅ AI Code WITH Accessibility Request:</div>
        <CodeBlock
          code={`// Accessible version - includes all users
button.addEventListener('click', handleStep);
button.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {
    if (e.key === 'Enter' || e.key === ' ') {
        e.preventDefault();
        handleStep();
    }
});

function handleStep() {
    currentIndex++;
    updateDisplay();
    announceChange();
}

function updateDisplay() {
    items.forEach((item, i) => {
        const isActive = i === currentIndex;
        item.style.background = isActive ? '#4CAF50' : '#fff';
        item.setAttribute('aria-current', isActive ? 'step' : 'false');
        // Text label, not just color
        item.querySelector('.status').textContent = isActive ? '→ Current' : '';
    });
}

function announceChange() {
    const announcement = document.getElementById('live-region');
    announcement.textContent = \`Now on step \${currentIndex + 1}\`;
}

/* ✅ Keyboard accessible with Enter and Space */
/* ✅ ARIA attributes for screen readers */
/* ✅ Text labels supplement color changes */`}
          lang='javascript'
        />
        <p class='code-comment mt-1'><strong>This code is safe.</strong> It works for everyone — mouse users, keyboard users, screen reader users, and people with color vision differences.</p>
      </div>

      <div class='hap-note-callout mt-3'>
        <div class='hap-note-content'>
          <h3>HAP's Accessibility Checklist: Non-Negotiable Rules</h3>
          <ul class='mt-1'>
            <li><strong>Always include "accessibility" in prompts</strong> — AI won't think of it on its own</li>
            <li><strong>List specific WCAG requirements</strong> — "keyboard accessible" is clearer than "accessible"</li>
            <li><strong>Specify what must NOT be removed</strong> — AI sees accessibility code as "complexity to simplify"</li>
            <li><strong>Test with keyboard after every AI change</strong> — if Tab doesn't work, something's broken</li>
            <li><strong>Verify color is not the only indicator</strong> — always add text labels or icons</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

