  <!-- ============================================================
         SECTION 9: HAP's Rules for Working with AI
         6 numbered rules (non-negotiable)
         ============================================================ -->
  <section class='content-section'>
    <div class='tips-container'>
      <h2 class='section-heading-centered'>HAP's Rules for Working with AI</h2>
      <p class='mb-2'>After months of working with AI for debugging control flow, I've developed six non-negotiable rules. These aren't suggestions — they're requirements that Prof. Teeters approves and enforces.</p>

      <div class='tip-card'>
        <div class='tip-number'>1</div>
        <div>
          <h3>Always Explain the Full Context</h3>
          <p>Never ask AI to "fix this code" without explaining what it should do, what it's currently doing wrong, and what constraints you're working with. I learned this when AI "fixed" my Fahrenheit code by converting it to Celsius — technically correct, completely wrong for my use case. Include: expected behavior, current behavior, environment details, and any constraints.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class='tip-card'>
        <div class='tip-number'>2</div>
        <div>
          <h3>Include What's Working, Not Just What's Broken</h3>
          <p>When I only showed AI the broken part, it sometimes "fixed" things by breaking the working parts. Now I always include surrounding code that must NOT change. I tell AI: "This function is called by X and Y, which depend on the return format. Don't change the return structure." Context prevents collateral damage.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class='tip-card'>
        <div class='tip-number'>3</div>
        <div>
          <h3>Request Accessibility Explicitly</h3>
          <p>AI will not automatically make code accessible. Every single prompt that involves user interaction must include accessibility requirements. I now have a standard section I paste into every prompt: "ACCESSIBILITY: keyboard accessible, visible focus states, screen reader support, color is not the only indicator." If I don't ask, I don't get.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class='tip-card'>
        <div class='tip-number'>4</div>
        <div>
          <h3>Verify Every Fix Manually</h3>
          <p>AI code that looks right isn't always right. I test every fix with: (1) the original failing case, (2) edge cases like empty arrays and zero values, (3) keyboard navigation, and (4) different data than AI used in its examples. Prof. Teeters says: "AI tests with imaginary data. You test with real data."</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class='tip-card'>
        <div class='tip-number'>5</div>
        <div>
          <h3>Understand Before You Ship</h3>
          <p>If I can't explain WHY a fix works, I don't ship it. I ask AI to explain its solution, then I verify that explanation makes sense. This caught a bug where AI used a regex that happened to work for my test case but would fail on real data. Understanding is not optional — it's how I take ownership.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class='tip-card'>
        <div class='tip-number'>6</div>
        <div>
          <h3>Keep a Debugging Log</h3>
          <p>I document what prompts worked, what failed, and what I learned. When I encounter a similar bug later, I have a reference. My log has entries like: "Falsy value bugs: must specify which falsy values to catch, !value is too broad." This compounds my learning over time.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================
         SECTION 10: Advanced Prompt Strategies
         Iteration, troubleshooting, safety prompts
         ============================================================ -->
  <section class='content-section'>
    <div class='topic-section'>
      <h2>Advanced Prompt Strategies</h2>
      <p class='mb-2'>Real AI collaboration isn't one-shot perfection — it's conversational refinement. Prof. Teeters calls this "conversational refinement" and it's how professionals actually work with AI.</p>

      <h3 class='mt-3'>Refinement Prompt</h3>
      <p class='mt-1'>When AI's first response is close but not quite right:</p>
      <div class='prompt-box mt-2'>
        <div class='prompt-label'>Refinement Prompt Template:</div>
        <CodeBlock
          code={`Your solution works but needs adjustment:

The fix you provided:
[paste AI's code]

What's not quite right:
[describe the issue - be specific]

Please revise to:
[specific change needed]

Keep everything else the same, especially [critical feature to preserve].`}
          lang='text'
        />
      </div>

      <h3 class='mt-3'>Debugging Prompt</h3>
      <p class='mt-1'>When AI's suggestion introduced a new bug:</p>
      <div class='prompt-box mt-2'>
        <div class='prompt-label'>Debugging Prompt Template:</div>
        <CodeBlock
          code={`Your suggested fix introduced a new problem:

Original bug: [what was wrong before]
Your fix: [paste the code AI gave you]
New problem: [what's wrong now - include error message if any]

The fix should:
- Solve the original bug
- NOT break [specific functionality that broke]
- Maintain [requirement that was violated]

Please provide a corrected version.`}
          lang='text'
        />
      </div>

      <h3 class='mt-3'>Safe Refactoring Prompt</h3>
      <p class='mt-1'>When you want AI to improve code without breaking it:</p>
      <div class='prompt-box mt-2'>
        <div class='prompt-label'>Safe Refactoring Prompt Template:</div>
        <CodeBlock
          code={`Refactor this code without breaking existing functionality.

PRESERVE (do not change):
- Return type and value format
- Parameter names and order
- Accessibility features (keyboard, focus, aria)
- All edge case handling

Current working code:
[paste your code]

Refactoring goal: [what you want improved - e.g., "more readable",
"better variable names", "fewer lines"]

Show me the refactored version and explain each change.`}
          lang='text'
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================
         SECTION 11: When NOT to Use AI
         3 scenarios where manual work is better
         ============================================================ -->
  <section class='content-section'>
    <div class='topic-section'>
      <h2>When NOT to Use AI</h2>
      <p class='mb-2'>AI is powerful for debugging, but it's not always the right tool. Understanding when NOT to use AI is just as important as knowing when to use it.</p>

      <div class='analysis-grid mt-2'>
        <div class='analysis-point'>
          <h3>When You're Learning New Concepts</h3>
          <p><strong>The struggle IS the learning.</strong> When I was first learning about truthy/falsy values in Station 1, I could have asked AI to explain everything. But Prof. Teeters encouraged me to make mistakes, get confused, and work through it myself. That frustration built understanding that no AI explanation could provide. Use AI to verify understanding AFTER you've struggled, not to skip the struggle entirely.</p>
        </div>

        <div class='analysis-point'>
          <h3>When Security Is Critical</h3>
          <p><strong>AI may introduce vulnerabilities it doesn't recognize.</strong> For authentication, authorization, or any code that handles sensitive data, AI suggestions need extra scrutiny. AI might suggest a "clean" solution that's actually insecure. Grace Hopper reminded me: "Security requires paranoia. AI is not paranoid — you must be." For security-critical code, get human review.</p>
        </div>

        <div class='analysis-point'>
          <h3>When You Need to Understand Your Own Logic</h3>
          <p><strong>If you don't understand it, you can't maintain it.</strong> Sometimes I have code that I wrote but don't remember how it works. The temptation is to ask AI to "explain and improve" it. But the better path is to trace through it myself, add comments as I understand it, and THEN decide if changes are needed. AI can explain code, but only you can understand YOUR code in YOUR context.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class='warning-box mt-3'>
        <h3>🟠 Prof. Teeters' Warning About AI and Learning</h3>
        <p class='mt-1'><strong>"HAP, AI can tell you the answer, but it can't teach you to think. Every time you skip the struggle, you skip the learning. Use AI to accelerate, not to replace your own thinking."</strong></p>
        <p class='mt-1'>She said this after I asked AI to solve a loop problem I hadn't even tried myself. I got a working answer but couldn't explain how it worked. That's when I learned: AI is for AFTER I've tried, not INSTEAD of trying.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

